WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:06.540
Stephen Hawking was born exactly 300 years
after the death of astronomer Galileo Galillei.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.130
Although this wasn’t a man who believed
in predestination.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:15.830
He was born in Oxford, England on January
8, 1942, to well-educated parents.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.880
Isobel Eileen Hawking earned her way to the
University of Oxford at a time when few women

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.180
got a degree.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:28.029
She studied economics, philosophy, and politics
and later worked as a medical research secretary.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:32.879
Frank Hawking also went to Oxford and studied
medicine, specializing in tropical diseases.

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:37.399
Frank’s research took them to London but
when Isobel became pregnant with Stephen,

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:40.719
she left the capital for Oxford to seek a
safer place.

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:45.620
London was still reeling from the aftermath
of the Blitz in 1941.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.439
After the birth of Stephen, two sisters followed,
Philippa and Mary

00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:52.180
as well as an adopted brother, Edward.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.559
Stephen began his education in Highgate, a
suburb of London, at the Byron House School,

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:58.829
a very progressive school.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:02.129
Hawking said the teachers didn’t believe
in drilling things into the students and he

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:06.280
blamed this on his inability to read until
he was eight years old.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.079
When he was eight, the family moved north
of London, to St Albans, Hertfordshire, when Frank became

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:15.700
head of the Department of Parasitology at
the National Institute for Medical Research.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.780
He wanted Stephen to go into medicine as well,
but his son was already enamored by the stars

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.399
and spent much of his time looking at the
sky and beyond.

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:25.780
The Hawkings were eccentric.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.109
Instead of chatting while having dinner, they
each sat silently reading a book.

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:33.929
They lived in a large, messy house and kept
bees in the basement.

00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:36.799
And they traveled around in an old London
taxi.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:40.159
Stephen’s family placed a high value on
education.

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:44.659
When he was 13, his father wanted him to get
into the highly regarded Westminster School,

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:46.780
immediately next to Westminster Abbey.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.580
But Stephen fell ill and didn’t sit for
a scholarship exam.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.289
Without it, they couldn’t afford the school
fees.

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:55.020
So he remained as a student at St Albans School.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.649
And perhaps that was a good thing.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:01.359
It was at St Albans that he met an inspirational
teacher, Dikran Tahta.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:05.850
Hawking said Tahta opened his eyes to the
blueprint of the universe: mathematics.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.060
Together, they built Hawking’s first computer
from old clock parts and a telephone switchboard.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:16.500
Hawking would say: “Behind every exceptional
person, there is an exceptional teacher.”

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.750
He originally wanted to study mathematics
at university but his father was worried that there

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.630
weren’t many jobs for math grads.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.769
Stephen would win a scholarship to study physics
at Oxford.

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:31.519
For his first 18 months as a university student,
he was bored and found the work ridiculously

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:32.519
easy.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.890
He was also lonely and didn’t have much
of a social life

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.459
That changed when he decided to join his college’s
boat club.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:42.709
His loud voice and lightweight made him the
perfect coxswain - the person in charge of

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:43.709
the boat.

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:47.780
A fellow boatman remarked that he often seemed
like he had “...his head in the stars, working

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.519
out mathematical formulae.”

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:52.290
But he didn’t put a lot of effort into his
studies.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:57.330
He said he spent only an hour a day on average
working during his entire time at Oxford.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:02.470
That made exams difficult and it nearly jeopardized
a first-class degree which he needed to get

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:06.120
admitted to the University of Cambridge for
postgraduate studies.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.120
His results were on the border between first
and second-class honours so he had to take

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.890
an oral exam.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.860
During the oral, he was asked to describe
his plans.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.560
He responded: "If you award me a First, I
will go to Cambridge.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.849
If I receive a Second, I shall stay in Oxford,
so I expect you will give me a First."

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:29.610
Hawking received a first-class Bachelor’s
degree in physics in 1962 and began grad work

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:33.010
at Cambridge in cosmology, the study of the
universe.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.019
He was disappointed to find he had been assigned
physicist Dennis Sciama as his supervisor

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:40.689
rather than the more famous astronomer Fred
Hoyle.

00:03:40.689 --> 00:03:43.450
However, this turned out to be a blessing
in disguise.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.720
Hoyle was rarely at the department while Sciama
was always eager to talk which helped stimulate

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.620
Hawking’s own scientific vision.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.049
Hoyle was also not a fan of the Big Bang theory
- the theory that the universe had a beginning

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:57.859
when it exploded with unimaginable force.

00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:01.730
He was the one who coined the term ‘big
bang’ to poke fun at the theory.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.400
On the other hand, Sciama was happy for Hawking
to investigate the beginning of time.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.549
But before he could delve deeply into his
research, he received devastating news.

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:15.490
He noticed he had been getting increasingly
clumsy during his time at Oxford.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.910
He fell down some stairs and the only suggestion
the doctor had was that he lay off the beer.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:26.820
However, in 1963, while at Cambridge, Hawking
was diagnosed with a motor neuron disease

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:33.500
called amyotrophic lateral sclerosis or ALS
which gradually paralyzes a person as the

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.090
brain can no longer control the muscles.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.970
Doctors gave him two years to live.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.990
He was just 21 years old.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.020
Hawking became deeply depressed.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.860
He felt there was no point in continuing his
studies.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:53.930
He wrote in his memoir: "I felt it was very
unfair — why should this happen to me?

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.600
At the time, I thought my life was over and
that I would never realise the potential I

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.320
felt I had."

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.680
However, the disease progressed slower than
expected.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.860
Sciama encouraged Hawking to return to his
research.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:11.080
Hawking’s greatest work revolutionized how
we think about black holes: a perfectly circular

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.690
region of space that’s formed when a star
collapses at the end of its life cycle.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.680
Albert Einstein’s General Theory of Relativity
had predicted black holes.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.810
The theory explains how gravity distorts spacetime.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:27.570
The gravitational pull of a black hole is
so strong that nothing can escape it, not

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:28.570
even light.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.340
And it warps the surroundings like a carnival
mirror.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:35.880
Einstein wasn’t entirely convinced that
these mysterious monsters existed.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.600
The work of British mathematician Sir Roger
Penrose proved general relativity really does

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.540
lead to the formation of black holes.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.930
And for that, he won a Nobel Prize, in 2020.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.760
Hawking became obsessed with black holes.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.080
And made a number of important discoveries
about them

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.700
including that they’re not entirely black.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.840
He discovered that they emit light just outside
the event horizon - which is the boundary

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.770
between normal space and the space affected
by the black hole’s gravity where nothing

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:02.810
can escape.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.240
He believed the slow leak of what’s now
known as Hawking radiation would cause the

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.810
black hole to evaporate over time.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.810
He had a knack for making bets about black
holes, too.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.710
He and physicist Kip Thorne teamed up to bet
that black holes swallow information, losing

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:22.440
it forever, while physicist John Preskill
thought that was nonsense and believed this

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.650
information would be recovered through the
radiation.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.360
This puzzle remains unresolved but Hawking
conceded the bet and as a concession gift,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.080
gave Preskill an encyclopedia of his choice:
one on baseball.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.900
Hawking used his research on black holes to
answer a bigger question: where did our universe

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:40.900
originate?

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:45.460
He took inspiration from Penrose’s work
which showed that deep inside a black hole,

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.420
a singularity exists.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.110
Where all conceptions of space and time break
down.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.170
Hawking applied the same thinking to the universe:
it also began in a singularity, adding credence

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:00.970
to the big bang theory that the universe began
at a single point in time.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:04.080
But that doesn’t mean he believed there
was a creator behind it all.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.660
He wrote: “Because there is a law such as
gravity, the universe can and will create

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:13.180
itself from nothing...It is not necessary
to invoke God to light the blue touch paper

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.740
and set the universe going.”

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.700
Blue touch paper is a reference to fireworks.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:22.670
That’s a different outlook than the father
of the Big Bang theory, who felt the law of

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.600
gravity was created by a creator.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.890
I talked about the life of Georges Lemaître,
a scientist and priest, in a previous video

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.420
which I’ll link in the description.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:33.860
Hawking was known to muse about God’s existence,
once telling Reuters: “The laws may have

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.520
been decreed by God, but God does not intervene
to break the laws.”

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.520
(Reuters 2007)

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.310
However, in the end, he was an atheist, although
he did fall in love with a Christian.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.860
Stephen met Jane Wilde at a party in their
hometown of St Albans.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.850
He, a physics student at Cambridge, she, an
English student at the University of London.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.970
They met around the time of his health prognosis.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.820
Hawking said Jane gave him something to live
for.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:02.650
They got married in 1965 and thought they
didn’t have a lot of time together.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.960
Turns out, they would be married for 30 years
and have 3 children.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.090
His health problems were a constant strain
on their marriage.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:14.390
Jane said she sought strength from her faith
as Stephen’s illness worsened and she became

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:18.820
his caregiver: feeding him, dressing him,
bathing him, being by his side through his

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.720
many hospital visits.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.460
But she also once said their marriage was
strained for another reason.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.040
She remarked: “The truth was, there were
four partners in our marriage.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.920
Stephen and me, motor neuron disease and physics.”

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:32.920
She remarked: “The truth was, there were
four partners in our marriage.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:33.920
Stephen and me,
motor neuron disease and physics.”

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.339
She felt shut out of his scientific world
where his work didn’t slow down despite

00:08:36.339 --> 00:08:38.089
his physical decline.

00:08:38.089 --> 00:08:42.259
When he lost his ability to write, he developed
unique ways of thinking.

00:08:42.259 --> 00:08:45.639
He had to rely on doing complicated equations
in his head.

00:08:45.639 --> 00:08:50.730
A physicist compared this to Mozart composing
an entire symphony in his mind!

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:55.350
Although the cruel disease robbed him of his
ability to move and slurred his speech, his

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.050
intellect remained sharp.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:02.800
He was elected to the Royal Society, the UK’s
national science academy, at the age of 32.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.940
And in 1979, he rose to become the Lucasian
Professor of Mathematics at Cambridge - the

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:12.680
most famous academic chair in the world - once
held by Sir Isaac Newton.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.339
He would give lectures and speeches with the
help of a computer-generated voice.

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:18.209
Can you hear me?

00:09:18.209 --> 00:09:21.880
He opted not to change his voice to a more
natural-sounding one later on because this

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.230
had already become his trademark.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:30.009
He didn’t actually lose his ability to speak
entirely until 1985 when he caught pneumonia

00:09:30.009 --> 00:09:32.759
while on a trip to a research center in Switzerland.

00:09:32.759 --> 00:09:35.920
He was so ill he had to be put on life support.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.139
When doctors asked his wife if they should
remove his ventilator, Jane refused.

00:09:40.139 --> 00:09:44.860
They had to cut a hole in his neck and place
a tube in his windpipe to help him breathe.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.960
The tracheotomy removed what was left of his
speech.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:52.550
In order to communicate, he chose letters
on a spelling card by raising his eyebrows.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:57.290
Later, he received a computer program from
American engineer Walter Woltosz (wall-toesszz),

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.550
whose mother suffered from ALS.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.240
Hawking could choose words from the database
with a click.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.480
But then he lost control of his hand and had
to resort to twitching his cheek muscle to

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:07.920
select the letters.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:11.959
This was painfully slow, and he could only
manage a word or two a minute.

00:10:11.959 --> 00:10:14.379
So he reached out to Intel for help.

00:10:14.379 --> 00:10:18.570
Researchers worked on a state-of-the-art system
that would interpret his intentions, kind

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.579
of like the iPhone’s predictive text feature.

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:26.170
Intel worked with London startup SwiftKey
on a word predictor which analyzed Hawking’s

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.310
papers to get a better idea of his writing
habits.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:32.610
For example, when he selected the word ‘black’
it automatically predicted that ‘hole’

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:33.620
would follow.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.920
It was thanks to technology that Hawking could
put his ground-breaking research into book

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:38.920
form.

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:41.760
He published his best-known work in 1988.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.921
A Brief History of Time sold a million copies
in just the first year - remarkable for a

00:10:46.921 --> 00:10:50.910
book about science - although many readers
didn’t finish it.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:56.149
It was very dense, even after Hawking omitted
all equations except one in order to appeal

00:10:56.149 --> 00:10:57.310
to the masses.

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:01.560
He only left in Einstein’s iconic e = mc2.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:06.009
Hawking was already well-known before the
book’s release but became a celebrity afterward.

00:11:06.009 --> 00:11:09.600
He appeared on the Simpsons, the Big Bang
theory, and Star Trek.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:13.730
He became a “cult-like” figure who also
had a mischievous streak.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:18.269
He was rumored to use his wheelchair to run
over the toes of people who annoyed him.

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:21.310
Prince Charles was apparently a victim.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:25.769
His fame also put both his professional and
personal life in the spotlight.

00:11:25.769 --> 00:11:29.990
Hawking ended up leaving Jane for one of the
nurses who cared for him, Elaine Mason.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:35.490
Mason’s husband was the engineer who attached
Hawking’s speech synthesizer to his wheelchair.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:39.569
Hawking said he grew more and more unhappy
at the relationship between Jane and Jonathan

00:11:39.569 --> 00:11:43.950
Jones, an organist at the local church, who
actually moved in with the Hawkings, when

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:48.540
Stephen was expected to die young, and Jane
wanted someone who could support the children.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:53.069
Jane married Jones after Hawking married Elaine
in 1995.

00:11:53.069 --> 00:11:56.370
His second marriage is said to have caused
friction with his children.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:59.990
They, too, would divorce after a decade together.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:05.069
And later resumed a closer relationship with
his first wife and his children.

00:12:05.069 --> 00:12:10.279
During an interview with Diane Sawyer on ABC
News, he talked about the most important advice

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:11.420
he gave his kids.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:16.130
He said: "One, remember to look up at the
stars and not down at your feet.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:17.891
Two, never give up work.

00:12:17.891 --> 00:12:22.649
Work gives you meaning, and purpose and life
is empty without it.

00:12:22.649 --> 00:12:29.029
Three, if you are lucky enough to find love,
remember it is rare and don't throw it away."

00:12:29.029 --> 00:12:31.230
And with the world, he once shared this:

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:37.089
“And however difficult life may seem, there
is always something you can do, and succeed

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:38.089
at.

00:12:38.089 --> 00:12:40.560
It matters that you don't just give up.”

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:44.339
He put it in another, more humorous way once
at the University of Oxford.

00:12:44.339 --> 00:12:49.860
If you feel you are in a black hole, don’t
give up

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:55.499
After 50 years of living with ALS, Hawking
passed away peacefully at his home, on March

00:12:55.499 --> 00:12:59.470
14, 2018, at the age of 76.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:02.259
His funeral took place at a church in Cambridge.

00:13:02.259 --> 00:13:06.820
Guests included the actors from the film about
his life, The Theory of Everything.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:11.020
The movie’s title is a reference to a single
theory that Hawking hoped to find to have

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.769
a complete understanding of the universe.

00:13:13.769 --> 00:13:16.230
One theory to explain how it all worked.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:20.889
It’s one of the major unsolved problems
in physics today.

00:13:20.889 --> 00:13:24.930
Despite all that he had accomplished there
were things he dreamt of doing but never did.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:29.800
He never got the chance to fly to space, although
he did travel aboard Zero Gravity’s Boeing

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:32.190
727 to experience weightlessness.

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:35.930
He was a strong advocate of space travel and
living on other planets.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:42.230
By the end of the century, I truly hope
humans will be living on Mars.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:46.889
He was seriously worried about what he saw
as potential threats to humanity: nuclear

00:13:46.889 --> 00:13:52.160
war, climate change, epidemics, asteroids,
and the rise of artificial intelligence.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.970
Hawking’s ashes were buried in Westminster
Abbey not far from the graves of two other

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.600
famous scientists, Newton and Charles Darwin.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.680
While he didn’t believe in predestination,
the brilliant scientist who was born on the

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:10.029
day Sir Isaac Newton died, died on the day
Albert Einstein was born.

00:14:10.029 --> 00:14:14.809
If you’ve been inspired by the life of Stephen
Hawking and want to learn more about how the

00:14:14.809 --> 00:14:19.170
universe works, my sponsor Brilliant can help
you improve your math, science, and computer

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:20.949
science skills.

00:14:20.949 --> 00:14:22.819
Brilliant is an interactive way to learn.

00:14:22.819 --> 00:14:27.600
In this course on scientific thinking, you
can explore the laws of physics as you play.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:31.820
Or you can learn about the major pillars of
calculus, like derivatives or integrals, through

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:33.199
